{"error":false,"activeTest":false,"task":{"id":14245006,"type":"Review","status":"Processed","importance":1,"reason":null,"locked":null,"proofId":null,"currentQuestionId":null,"currentQuestionTimestamp":null,"progress":1,"points":4,"passed":1,"rawPointsAwarded":6,"pointsAwarded":6,"mp":null,"mpAwarded":null,"mpDecayRate":null,"student":{"id":57975,"firstName":"Harrison","lastName":"Charles","locale":null,"subscriptionId":"sub_1UO3yHD4gcTFRx97dYjQXmX1","sequenceId":19},"course":{"id":111,"name":"Mathematical Foundations II"},"topic":{"id":2336,"name":"Factoring Biquadratic Expressions"},"test":{"id":null,"name":null,"diagnostic":null,"timeLimit":null,"questionCount":null},"created":"2026-10-15T15:20:18.000Z","started":"2026-10-15T15:24:57.000Z","completed":"2026-10-15T15:25:46.000Z","processed":"2026-10-15T15:26:05.000Z","progressStr":"100%","answers":[{"id":62278934,"created":"2026-10-15T15:25:10.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12544,"number":1,"createdStr":"Thu, Oct 15th @ 3:25 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:13","question":{"id":36118,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":5019,"text":"<p>Given that $6x^6-13x^3-8=\\left(ax^3-8\\right)\\left(2x^3+b\\right)$ where $a$ and $b$ are integers, find the value of $a+b.$</p>\n","choice1":"$4$","choice2":"$10$","choice3":"$6$","choice4":"$2$","choice5":"$7$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>We can introduce a new variable $z=x^3$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align*}\n6x^6-13x^3-8\t&= 6\\left(x^3\\right)^2-13\\left(x^3\\right)-8\\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t&= 6z^2 - 13z - 8\n\\end{align*}</p>\n<p>Now, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $x\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\t6z^2 - 13z - 8\t&= (3z - 8)(2z + 1)  \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t&=\\left( 3x^3 - 8 \\right)\\left( 2x^3 + 1 \\right)\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>So, $a=3$, $b=1$ and $a+b=4.$</p>\n","topic":{"id":2336,"name":"Factoring Biquadratic Expressions"},"example":{"id":5019,"title":"Factoring Biquadratic Expressions as Quadratic Trinomials With Leading Coefficients"},"group":{"id":156414,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[{"id":null,"number":null,"answers":[]}],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14245006,"answer":{"id":62278934,"cread":"2026-10-15T15:25:10.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12544},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62278951,"created":"2026-10-15T15:25:28.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12165,"number":2,"createdStr":"Thu, Oct 15th @ 3:25 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:12","question":{"id":44522,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":5018,"text":"<p>The trinomial $p^4 + 2p^2 - 15$ is equivalent to</p>\n","choice1":" $\\left( p^2 - 3 \\right)\\left( p^2 + 5 \\right)$","choice2":" $\\left( p^2 + 3 \\right)\\left( p^2 - 5 \\right)$","choice3":"$\\left( p^2 - 10 \\right)\\left( p^2+ 5 \\right)$","choice4":"$\\left( p^2+ 5 \\right)^2$","choice5":" $\\left( p - 3 \\right)\\left( p + 5 \\right)^2$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>We can introduce a new variable $z=p^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\np^4 + 2p^2 - 15\t&= \\left(p^2\\right)^2+2\\left(p^2\\right) - 15 \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t&= z^2 + 2z - 15\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Now, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $p\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tz^2 + 2z - 15\t&= (z - 3)(z + 5)  \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t&= \\left( p^2 - 3 \\right)\\left( p^2 + 5 \\right)\n\\end{align}</p>\n","topic":{"id":2336,"name":"Factoring Biquadratic Expressions"},"example":{"id":5018,"title":"Factoring Biquadratic Expressions as Quadratic Trinomials"},"group":{"id":13817,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14245006,"answer":{"id":62278951,"cread":"2026-10-15T15:25:28.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":12165},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62278979,"created":"2026-10-15T15:25:45.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":13437,"number":3,"createdStr":"Thu, Oct 15th @ 3:25 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:13","question":{"id":79490,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":5018,"text":"<p>Factor $s^4 - s^2 - 12.$</p>\n","choice1":"$(s^2+3)(s^2-4)$","choice2":"$(s^2-3)(s^2-4)$","choice3":"$(s^2-3)(s^2+4)$","choice4":"$(s+3)(s-4)$","choice5":"$(s-3)(s+4)$","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p>We can introduce a new variable $t=s^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $t\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\ts^4 - s^2 - 12 &=\\left(s^2\\right)^2 - \\left(s^2\\right) - 12 \\\\[3pt]\n\t &= t^2 - t - 12\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>Now, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $s\\mathbin{:}$</p>\n<p>\\begin{align}\n\tt^2 - t - 12 &=\t(t+3)(t-4)  \\\\[3pt]\n\t &=(s^2+3)(s^2-4)\n\\end{align}</p>\n","topic":{"id":2336,"name":"Factoring Biquadratic Expressions"},"example":{"id":5018,"title":"Factoring Biquadratic Expressions as Quadratic Trinomials"},"group":{"id":13817,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14245006,"answer":{"id":62278979,"cread":"2026-10-15T15:25:45.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":13437},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}}],"completedStr":"Completed @ 3:25 PM","localGraph":{"topicId":2336,"topics":[{"id":382,"name":"Factoring Perfect Square Trinomials With Leading Coefficients","prerequisites":[],"frontier":0,"halfLife":5.286776884499879,"rep